guns <sungmustpae@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> On my machine at least, spfx should then have 32 bits and therefore
> four more places to stuff special flags, right? It seems to work for
> me,

and also for SporkHack which added three more SPFX_* constants.

> but I am wondering if there's some portability thing that I'm not
> thinking about, or some Nethack quirk that caused the Devteam to
> restrict the SPFX bitfield to 28 bits?

The C89 standard seems to define that a byte has at least 8 bits and a
long 32 bits. So nowadays you should be on the safe side.

I don't know for sure, but AFAIK there never was a C-compiler that
used bytes of 7 bit length or longs with 28 bits. But I wouldn't be
surprised of compilers with 16 bit longs.

I guess the "restriction" to 28 bit was not by portability consideration
but by design. They probably just didn't know what else artifacts
should get.

